      "answer_markdown": "Lunar base: 1.62 m/s² (0.165 g), 8.2 psia, 34% O₂, vent 10 cm/s, flame size 10 mm.\n\n**Regime: mixed.** For Lunar base the buoyant velocity is 25.5 cm/s against a vent flow of 10 cm/s, so the Richardson number Ri = u_b²/u_f² is 6.48 (forced Ri < 0.1, buoyant Ri > 10) [eng:physics]. Sampling the uncertain constants gives 0% forced, 71.7% mixed and 28.3% buoyant flames [eng:physics].\n\nIn the mixed regime neither the 0 g (ISS, forced-flow) data nor 1 g (buoyant) data apply directly: buoyancy and the vent flow are comparable, which is exactly the condition PSI never tested.\n\nDesign implication: At lunar gravity a 10 mm flame stays forced-flow dominated (Ri < 0.1, where microgravity evidence transfers) only above about 80 cm/s of vent flow at the median coefficients, and above 139 cm/s to cover 95% of the Monte Carlo samples (u_b p50 25.4 cm/s, p95 43.8 cm/s). Typical cabin vents (5-20 cm/s) are far below this, so lunar fires are mixed or buoyancy-dominated. [eng:design:1]\n\nConsequence for the predictions: **M1 – P(droplet flame self-extinguishes before burnout)**: estimate 20.1%, 90% band 1.25% to 68.4% [eng:M1]. The microgravity-only band is 3.9% to 48.2%. Transfer confidence: **low**.",
